module bad
  purpose: Declarations a foreign module may not hold: each one breaks a rule of the boundary.

import std.foreign exposing Int8, Int32
import std.filesystem exposing Path

public function console_too(value: Int32) returns Int32 needs console, foreign
  purpose: `int console_too(int value)`, needing more than `foreign`.

public function failing(value: Int32) returns Int32 or fails with Text needs foreign
  purpose: `int failing(int value)`, declaring a failure.

public function generic(value: Item) returns Item needs foreign for any Item
  purpose: `int generic(int value)`, taking a type parameter.

public function listed(values: List of Int32) returns Int32 needs foreign
  purpose: `int listed(int *values)`: a list does not cross the boundary.

public function pathed(path: Path) needs foreign
  purpose: `void pathed(const char *path)`: a Path does not cross the boundary.

public function sized() returns Bytes needs foreign
  purpose: `unsigned char *sized(void)`: no C function reports the length of its bytes.

public function wide(aa: Int8, bb: Int8, cc: Int8, dd: Bytes, ee: Int8, ff: Int8) needs foreign
  purpose: `void wide(...)`: seven words, one over the limit.
